Implements ADR 0008. Order matters -- each step is useful on its own.

1. Node tags. Add a tags field, resolve tag selectors to node-id lists at config load. This alone removes the per-request node-list scan and hash copy in _eligible_nodes.
2. Model aliases + tiers. Client-facing name -> ordered tier list, each tier a tag selector plus its own wait_ms. Fall-through on failed admission, not failed eligibility.
3. Policy profiles + default_policy + per-key exceptions, resolved once at config load into shared immutable objects. Keys not listed take the default, so N identical customers cost zero entries.
4. Failure codes stay distinct: unknown alias 503, alias not granted 403, all permitted tiers saturated 429.

Hard requirement with its own test: a tier a policy forbids must be INVISIBLE to eligibility for that key, not merely deprioritised. The test exists to prove data cannot reach an excluded cloud.

Usage events must carry both the requested alias and the served model, or cost attribution breaks silently.
Step 1 DONE: node tags. tags on nodes (normalized: lowercase, trim, dedupe, order-preserving, accepts list or 'a, b' string), select_nodes / nodes.select with AND semantics, tags argument on pick_node / route_state / route.next / route.state, tags part of the route key so selections do not share a round-robin cursor. Derived node lists cached, invalidated by a generation counter bumped on every inventory change (including a direct assignment through the nodes accessor, caught by a Moo trigger). t/24-node-tags.t, 31 assertions, verified to fail when the invalidation is removed.

MEASURED, and the performance justification did NOT hold: at 8 nodes the cache makes no difference (127.3 vs 126.5 req/s, less than run-to-run spread). Only at 100 nodes is it visible (~4%: 127.5/126.5 vs 122.5). ADR 0008 corrected -- the cache is kept for tier fall-through and the policy layer, not because routing was slow.

Remaining: step 2 (aliases + tiers), step 3 (policies + default_policy + per-key exceptions), step 4 (403/429/503 separation).
Step 2 DONE: model aliases + tiers.

set_model_alias / route_plan / alias.set / route.plan. An alias is an ordered list of tiers; each tier is a tag selector + the model to ask those nodes for + its own wait_ms (default 0) + optional engine. A model with no alias resolves to a single implicit tier on its own name with the global wait, so aliasless configs are byte-for-byte unchanged.

_begin_route_async walks the plan: no eligible node in a tier -> skip immediately (waiting cannot conjure a node); eligible but unadmitted -> wait out that tier's window, then fall through; plan exhausted -> 503 if no tier was ever eligible, 429 if one was. Verified by timing in t/25: 4ms fall-through at wait_ms 0, 256ms at wait_ms 250, 3ms past a dead tier with a 5s window.

Requested vs served model is now real: the upstream body carries the served model, the usage event carries both. requested_model added to both shipped schemas plus an ALTER-on-prepare migration in UsageStore::DBI, because CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS does nothing to an existing table and the failure mode is silently missing billing data. Regression test in t/12 builds an old-schema table and upgrades it.

Suite: 259 tests via dzil test.

Remaining for this ticket: step 3 (policy profiles, default_policy, per-key exceptions, resolved-and-interned at config load) and step 4 (403 for an alias a key may not use). Note step 4 needs step 3 -- there is no per-key grant to check yet.
Steps 3 and 4 DONE -- ticket complete.

Policy profiles + default_policy + per-key exceptions, resolved once at config load. A key entry is a profile name, or a hash with 'policy' plus SPARSE overrides (absent field keeps the profile's value). Unlisted keys take default_policy, so N identically-configured customers cost zero entries. Identical resolutions are interned by fingerprint, so keys on one profile share one object (asserted via refaddr). Naming an undefined policy croaks at load -- failing open would hand out access.

route_plan returns { tiers, permitted, reason }. permitted => 0 covers model_not_permitted and all_tiers_denied; both render 403 permission_error. Saturation of PERMITTED capacity stays 429 -- no fall-through to a denied node when the allowed ones are full, which is the failure the whole design exists to prevent.

The hard requirement is met in both halves: deny_tags filters node SELECTION as well as dropping tiers from the plan. Tier-level only would have been theatre -- a key denied 'cloud' could reach a cloud node by asking for its raw model name instead of the alias.

FOUND AND FIXED while testing it, a real hole this ticket would otherwise have left wide open: _request_api_key_id believed the client's x-skeid-key-id header. Until now that was 'only' invoice forgery; with policies it becomes 'pick your own permissions in one header line'. The id is now derived from the presented key (Langertha::Skeid->key_id_for_key), and the header is honoured only under routing.trust_key_id_header for deployments that authenticate in front of Skeid. New 'skeid keyid' CLI prints the id, because a config must be able to name a customer without holding their key.

t/26-key-policies.t, 50 assertions, verified to fail under two separate sabotages: removing the node-level deny (5 failures) and always trusting the header (3 failures). Suite: 309 tests via dzil test.

